WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.660
Every long conversation with an AI model
creates a quiet problem.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.770
The model has to remember what came before.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.420
Not as sentences, but as thousands,
then millions,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.620
of high-dimensional vectors.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.600
Those vectors sit inside
the key-value cache,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.130
the working memory that lets a transformer
keep context.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.300
And as context windows grow,

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.490
this cache can become one of the most
expensive parts of inference.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.280
TurboQuant, from Google Research,
attacks that bottleneck directly:

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.380
not by making the model smaller,

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.630
but by making its memory
dramatically cheaper.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.670
To understand TurboQuant,
start with vectors.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.360
A vector is the basic way
modern AI systems represent meaning.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.220
A word, an image patch, a search result,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.350
or a piece of context becomes a point
in a high-dimensional space.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.970
Similar ideas land near each other.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.390
Different ideas drift apart.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.700
This geometric trick powers semantic
search, retrieval-augmented generation,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.740
and the attention mechanism
inside large language models.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.320
But geometry has a price.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.100
If every point needs many precise
numbers, memory grows fast.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.000
The more context the model…
